# Chapter 16: Ambush at the Oort Cloud

Leo grappled with the slick metallic rim of the turret's gunner station, hauling himself upright behind the flickering console. Astrid's curt briefing still echoed in his ears, but his gut instincts screamed it to be wrong—and they had yet to receive any confirmation of successful acquisition via the probes or long-range scans. The damned things were notoriously unreliable in such dense asteroid fields. "Negative readings," he radioed curtly. "Everything looks dead."

"Hrrngh!" A low growl of frustration rumbled in her throat as she yanked free of the hatch, slapping controls shut behind her. Sealant gas hissed as the breach inflated, restoring pressure. Alarms flashed across the command center board, demanding attention to hull breaches along three exposed decks. The structural integrity monitor whined ominously under the stresses. With a deep breath, Astrid unclipped the holstered rifle hanging by her chair.

"All stations report in!" came her clipped command. "Status of defensive systems? Are we still—"

The emergency klaxon wailed stridently, cutting her off. Explosive impacts battered the ship's flank, throwing off shields and rocking her in its cradle of debris. Warning lights flashed red as the inertial dampeners strained to compensate.

"What the _fuck—_?" Leo snapped, straining for an outside view. His heart dropped as the shattered remains of several asteroids drifted into view, trailing fire, smoke, and the detritus of obliteration.

"Looks like we've got company," Astrid gritted, her gaze darting across scanner displays. Multiple ships, closing fast—

Dominion Navy, by the looks of their distinctive prow turrets. Traitorous bastards must've been on Syndicate payroll after all. The trap had been waiting patiently among the scattered ruins, invisible until now...

Leo felt the first wave of shock giving way to grim acceptance. All those careful precautions, the carefully laid plans—they'd bought themselves some time, yes...but not enough. Now the Dominion Navy had pounced, exploiting the void's cruel mathematics to catch the Marauder unprepared.

The tactical override screen flashed urgently. Thrusters roared to full burn as the Marauder lunged into evasive maneuvers, compensating for gravity well forces while reasserting control over damaged thruster clusters. Heat shivered across the matte-black hull, radiating away into inky blackness where stars spun wildly outside the viewing ports.

"Brace for impact!" Astrid yelled over the cacophony raging through comms. Leaning forward on shaking knees, Leo locked his gaze on her face, bracing for the next salvo...

✦ ✦ ✦

Relic of the Void
Chapter 16: Ambush at the Oort Cloud
Scene 2

"Fuck. Me." Leo choked out between clenched teeth, his fists white-knuckled on the armrests. The Marauder shook and rattled like a tossed tin can as another barrage clipped past their shields, sending shuddering jolts through the hull. Alarms shrieked and klaxons hammered eardrums raw. Outside, the hellish light show of near misses painted the viewing portals in frenetic strobes.

Astrid grunted, ducking reflexively as another explosion rocked them sideways. Her silver hair billowed around her furious face, streaked with blood and soot. She spared one wild-eyed glance at Leo before snapping her focus back to the holographic tactical display.

"They're relentless," she spat. "Must have expected us."

Leo's jaw clenched tight enough to crack enamel. He met her eyes directly, refusing to squirm under that piercing gaze. "Would I put it past those slimy Syndicate scum? They didn't strike me as above dirty tactics."

"That's generous," Astrid snarled back, voice tinged with vicious amusement. "Assuming your twenty-first century upbr—"

"F**k my upbringin', Vex! I lived through this crap, remember?"

The interjecting barks of proximity alarms cut short any retorts they might've exchanged. The Starlight Marauder lurched viciously as a broadside slammed into their port flank, crumpling metal and sparking plasma. A deafening clang rang through the ship as armored plates burst open, releasing caustic air hissed into vacuum. Warning lights flickered wildly, throwing jagged shadows across Astrid and Leo's frantic faces.

"Hull breach on Deck Six!" the AI announced imperiously, its melodic cadence warped by static and echoing distress. Leo's gut turned upside down as he imagined crewmates venting explosively into the void. In this claustrophobic steel tomb, every inch of metal now counted against the onslaught beating their defenses.

Astrid cursed, her eyes blazing with icy fury—the cold rage only those who'd faced such fathomless horrors could muster. "Don't look back, Mercer. Save that shit-eatin' noble pride for when you're lyin' on a funeral pyre talkin' to ghost-friends about what a hero y—"

"I don't do heroes!" Leo bit off in the same instant a blinding nova of plasma lanced through the ruined canopy above them, bathing the cockpit in searing crimson. He jerked back instinctively, shielding his eyes against the afterimage as his skin began to sizzle with heat. "I'm a survivor, and if I gotta carve out a place in this pitiless universe, I'll goddamn do it!"

"You are something else, ain't ya?" Astrid growled past gritted teeth, ripping clear her battle-mask without taking her attention off navigational plot. "Cocky, arrogant - but you've _got balls,_ Mercer. Balls made of pure, dumb steel. And I hate that about you."

"Well color me flattered," Leo shot back, wincing as his scorched cheeks stung. "Just so long as those steely orbs of mine keep us breathing beyond the next few microseconds, capisce?"

Rolls and crunches echoed ominously through the ship's skeletal frame as heavy rounds pounded at their shields like angry gods trying to smite mortal sinners.

✦ ✦ ✦

The frigid metal of the blast shield still scorched Leo's cheek when another brutal smack cracked against the outer hull plating, sending a concussion wave rippling through his body like a hammer blow. Searing air hissed from the breaches, each new volley eroding precious centimeters of armor. Time seemed to blur into kaleidoscopic chaos - a screaming tornado of fire and noise.

But he was alive, crouched in the relative sanctuary of the flight deck. That counted for something, even as reality crumbled around them. Across the cramped cabin, Vex peered intently at the main viewer, face lit by the strobing warnings flickering across consoles.

"Cargo bay breach confirmed, we've lost containment! Pressure dropping fast," her cultured contralto voice reported flatly, devoid of panic despite the desperate urgency in her jade-green eyes. Smoke curled lazily from damaged circuitry, sizzling lines tangled across charred insulation.

Leo's gaze darted over their perilous predicament, racing to prioritize tasks amidst the mounting disaster. He glanced frantically at the navigation readouts overlain on the transparent display -- grim data painting a grim picture.

"We've taken too much damage to pull this off in one pass, Vex. We need to..." His statement dissolved into splintered shrieks of tortured metal as an unrelenting barrage pounded the stern section. Concussive force rocked the crippled fighter, hurling Leo hard against the backrest of his command chair with a choking gasp.

Through haze of dizziness, he glimpsed her reaction -- fierce resolve burning in those catlike emerald irises, jaw clenched. In that moment, fear for himself receded, overtaken by concern for her well-being amidst the maelstrom they found themselves entangled in.

Vex had already begun typing commands on her console, fingers barely visible as they danced across keypads. Leo's foggy mind struggled to follow her rapid inputs: "Auxiliary thrusters online... compensating for asymmetrical damage... setting up evasive maneuvers." Her words were a stream of barely intelligible white noise amidst the screams of warping alloys.

The crippled fighter lurched violently to port as her adjustments kicked in, throwing him sideways against the restraints that would never tighten enough to hold him in position. Alarms shrieked and flashed a sickening cadence of crimson along every console edge, casting an eerie glow on Vex's grim features.

Leo's throat constricting, he managed to croak, "We can't outrun this...not with engines this hobbled. Think!"

His plea was drowned out by another earsplitting crash, this time more ominous, bearing an unmistakable resonance. Shudders that began at the rear bulkheads traveled forward, building toward a crescendo that shook Leo's bones. Vex's head whipped around as she snatched at the warning displayed on her screen: "Structural failure imminent...we—"

With a thunderous roar that vaporized what remained of their hearing, the starboard wing disintegrated in a cascade of flaming wreckage. Flipped onto its side, the crippled fighter careened wildly, spinning into an uncontrollable tumble down a death spiral.

Clutching frantically for handholds, Leo felt like a ragdoll tossed in a washing machine as turbulence pummeled him mercilessly from every direction. Gravity's pull vanished, leaving him sense-deprived, stomach lurching up his throat with no ground to press against.

On the verge of blacking out from G-forces and sensory overload, Vex's anguished scream cut through the cacophony.

✦ ✦ ✦

Space blurred past the cockpit windows, a kaleidoscope of stars and debris streaking through the void in a mad dance of disintegrating reality. The fighter spun violently, impossible to control, careening down a reckless parabolic course destined to end in fiery destruction. Vex fought her console, desperate to stabilize the craft, even though she knew it was futile. Her hair whipped around her face, framing eyes that gleamed with defiance, lips pulled back in a feral snarl beneath the scar tissue mapped across her cheeks. 

Leo clung to his restraints with white-knuckled desperation, feeling the deck plates vibrate ominously underfoot as catastrophic structural damage took its toll. Panic clawed at his chest, squeezing the air from his lungs until he gasped for oxygen like a fish out of water. The constant, bone-jarring wrenches threatened to shake the life from him, leaving him helpless and vulnerable. He watched, mesmerized, as Vex worked feverishly, her brow furrowed in concentration and rage mingling across her face. Her skin was slick with perspiration despite the near-freezing temperatures within the cramped cockpit. A faint copper scent lingered in the recycled air, a reminder of the adrenaline-pumping urgency driving them both.

In the midst of this chaos, a blinding glare pierced the darkness outside - an immense, swirling vortex of superheated dust and gas, surrounded by a halo of incandescent blue light. The behemoth nebula filled their view, its core pulsing with an otherworldly intensity that seared retina and soul alike. Vex spared a glance towards it, jaw clenched in frustration as if unwilling to lose focus even for a moment.

"We need to-" She interrupted herself mid-sentence with a pained grunt, swiping futilely at her controls after another violent impact rattled the stricken fighter. More consoles winked out, systems failing one by one under the relentless barrage.

Just then, the ship rocked again, jolting them sideways as another loud bang echoed through the hull. "What the fuck was that?" Leo shouted, struggling to regain equilibrium amidst the disorienting tumbling. Outside, a massive chunk of smoldering wreckage drifted lazily past their canopy, trailing strands of molten steel like a grisly comet tail. Its erratic path hinted at a second, larger craft - likely the source of the devastating attack they couldn't see.

"Well, damn. Looks like we found those bastards," Vex growled, her tone devoid of humor even as resignation settled heavy on her features. She reached across the narrow gap separating them, fingers brushing lightly against Leo's where they gripped the harness. Despite the precariousness of their situation, the gesture sent a pulse of warmth though him, a flicker of connection amidst the apocalyptic backdrop. "Hope your ride knows some crazy aerobatics...if you want to survive this shit show."

A burst of static filled the radio, voices garbled and distant. Then recognition sharpened as commands cut through the chatter, identifying the channel caller as none other than Rigel Station's emergency dispatch. "...fighters reported hostile boarding attempt, multiple casualties...Raptor squad dispatched, ETA several minutes, coordinate..." Vex's eyes narrowed, processing the intel rapidly even in the depths of impending doom. If the station's forces could reach them before—

Another deafening boom shook the fighter, this time hitting more squarely amidships. For a stark, shining moment, all motion ceased, as if suspended in time.